            <div xml:id="d394">

                <pb facs="0811" n="725" sameAs="frus:frus1941-43/pg725"/>

                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">Editorial Note</hi>
                </head>
                <p>No official record of the substance of this conversation has been found. The
                    information set forth above is derived from the Log, <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >ante</hi>, <ref target="#pg533">p. 533</ref>, and Hopkins’ notes of January
                    24, <hi rendition="#italic">post</hi>, <ref target="#pg839">p. 839</ref>. The
                    brief accounts of the meeting by Elliott Roosevelt (p. 120), de Gaulle (pp.
                    94–95), Catroux (p. 322), Giraud (pp. 109–110), and Macmillan (p. 253) indicate
                    that the principal subject of discussion was the joint statement to the press to
                    be made by de Gaulle and Giraud. It appears to have been agreed that the two
                    French leaders would prepare such a statement after Roosevelt and Churchill had
                    left Casablanca. Macmillan (p. 254) recalls that the statement was prepared late
                    on the afternoon of January 24; for the text, issued to the press on January 26,
                    see de Gaulle, <hi rendition="#italic">Documents</hi>, p. 94. After the
                    conversation, the four leaders moved to the lawn at the rear of the President’s
                    villa for photographs; see <hi rendition="#italic">ante</hi>, <ref
                        target="#pg483">p. 483</ref>.</p>
            </div>
